    <entry>
        <id>https://agilesysadmin.github.io/2024/07/25/packaging-go-dependencies-for-gentoo/</id>
        <title>Packaging Go dependencies for Gentoo</title>
        <author><name>Ferenc Erki</name></author>
        <link href="https://agilesysadmin.github.io/2024/07/25/packaging-go-dependencies-for-gentoo/" rel="alternate" />
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[
            <p>While I don’t write Go code, I do package Go projects for Gentoo Linux. My
previous post about how to <a href="https://agilesysadmin.github.io/2023/04/24/host-gentoo-dependency-tarballs-as-github-releases/">Host Gentoo dependency tarballs as GitHub
releases</a> got
me invited to give a talk at the <a href="https://www.meetup.com/golang-users-berlin/events/300809374/">Google Developer Group Berlin Golang
meetup</a>, to host
a community round table discussion at GopherCon Europe 2024, and to hold an
online workshop for <a href="https://gentoo-ev.org/">Gentoo e.V.</a></p>

<p>My presentation expands on the above post, and I decided to share the extra
parts in this follow-up, including an idea for Go project maintainers who wish
to streamline the distribution of their work.</p>

                <p><a href="https://agilesysadmin.github.io/2024/07/25/packaging-go-dependencies-for-gentoo/#section-2">Continue reading...</a></p>

                <p>Tags:
                    <a href="https://agilesysadmin.github.io/tag/golang/">golang</a>
                    <a href="https://agilesysadmin.github.io/tag/gentoo/">gentoo</a>
                </p>

        ]]></content>
        <updated>2024-07-25T00:00:00Z</updated>
        <category term="golang" />
        <category term="gentoo" />
    </entry>
